              ><div class="iJ9abRlUxGQAmfFMc" data-instant-type="container"
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text ieUX3S0pBupZq4csM"><p>2020</p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text isgMkmIUDZxdmcvYB"><p>I discovered it was used thousands of years ago</p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text icyxeodC4eQys9oql"><p>I started researching. Deep diving into behavioral psychology, addiction patterns, ancient wellness practices.</p><p></p><p>That&apos;s when I found it.</p><p></p><p>Ancient cultures, 4,000 years ago, used aromatic plant inhalation not as medicine, but as ritual.</p><p></p><p>They understood something we&apos;d forgotten: the ceremony matters more than the substance.</p><p></p><p>We didn&apos;t need to invent something new. We needed to reclaim what they knew all along.</p></div></div
